# etude-s6z.1 plan — bridge live stages to Claude producers

Track: full STRICT loop. This changes live CLI behavior and passes an
authentication credential to an external producer process.

ANCESTOR: `etude-s6z` (P2) proves four derivation stages can run live through
real LLM producers. This bead supplies the missing producer adapter measured by
a configured plan-stage execution that produces a non-empty LLM artifact.

MEASUREMENT: every stage currently resolves the registry `dev` seat to
`claude -p --model opus` with no prompt argument, while `ExecRunner` discards
stdout and requires `$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE`; therefore every configured live stage
fails with `output missing`.

BLAST RADIUS: repository config and runner behavior inside this worktree;
authenticated Claude calls explicitly launched for verification; Claude CLI's
normal configuration/cache state beneath allowlisted `HOME`; and unreferenced
blob, tree, and commit objects written to the repository's shared Git object
database for the dirty-tree live proof. The wrapper passes
`--no-session-persistence`, suppressing resumable session transcript state for
this bead; `etude-o9z` will deliberately revisit that mode when it captures
session identity and logs. No refs are moved by temporary object construction.
No host packages, system directories outside Claude's user state, containers,
shared services, or other lane files are changed.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCES: sending the prompt to Claude can incur a remote request
and charge. The wrapper performs all local validation and prompt construction
before that send. Claude CLI offers no provider idempotency key or durable
pre-send protocol, so the logical stage has no exactly-once guarantee.

CRASH-PREFIX RESIDUALS:

| Crash prefix | Durable/local residual | External effect | Retry effect | Observable evidence | Completion |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| before prompt construction completes | partial scratch prompt and possibly an empty sibling stdout temp, removed on normal exit only | none | safe; no charge | scratch files until scratch cleanup | incomplete |
| prompt complete, before Claude accepts stdin | complete scratch prompt plus empty sibling stdout temp | none | safe; no charge | scratch files | incomplete |
| after request send, before Claude exits | prompt plus possible partial temp stdout | request may execute and charge | can issue and charge a duplicate request | provider/CLI diagnostics; no stage artifact | incomplete |
| Claude exits 0, before validation/rename | prompt plus complete temp stdout | completed charged request | crash retry can duplicate the request | complete temp file in scratch | incomplete |
| after atomic rename, before engine capture | prompt plus renamed scratch output; after host power loss the rename is not fsync-durable, so output may be absent but is never treated as a captured artifact | completed charged request | engine/operator retry can duplicate the request | output may exist locally but run ref does not record it | wrapper complete; stage incomplete |
| after engine captures output | run-ref artifact plus normal scratch residuals until cleanup | completed charged request | no retry required; an explicit rerun is a new charged request | run manifest and artifact | complete |

Normal non-crash failures are not retried inside the wrapper. A trap removes
prompt and unpublished output temporaries on normal shell exit; host/process
crashes can leave them for the engine's scratch cleanup.

GLOBAL REQUEST SEQUENCE: a complete default workflow is sequential, not one
request total. Each of the five stage wrappers can add exactly one producer
request; after each producer artifact the existing gate engine can invoke the
configured reviewer seats (and a fallback only when its primary fails) before
the next producer runs. Thus this change introduces at most five producer sends
per uninterrupted full run, while reviewer sends are pre-existing gate effects.
The live plan proof can incur the plan producer request followed by the two L2
reviewer primaries and any configured failure fallbacks. A crash at any prefix
can preserve already-captured earlier artifacts/gates on the run ref while
losing the current scratch output; resume may skip captured work, whereas an
explicit fresh run or rerun can repeat any prior charged request. There is no
cross-request exactly-once guarantee. Per-request residuals are the table above;
the durable boundary between requests is the engine's run-ref capture.

TOPOLOGY: one `ExecRunner` process invokes one wrapper and one Claude CLI per
stage. `ExecRunner.Run` resolves `WorktreeDir`, assigns it to `cmd.Dir`, and its
existing `TestExecRunner_CWD` regression proves relative commands and agent
lookups execute inside that pinned worktree. There is no new concurrent writer;
the detached worktree remains disposable and the stage artifact is committed
to the run ref only by the existing engine after the wrapper succeeds.

DURABILITY CLASS: non-idempotent remote effect, at-most-once per wrapper
process, with no exactly-once guarantee across a logical-stage retry. The
wrapper creates the unpublished temporary as a sibling in the exact
`$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE` directory, requires it to be non-empty, and renames it
atomically on that filesystem only after Claude exits successfully; retry
semantics remain owned by the existing live engine and gate loop.

COMPLETENESS ENUMERATION: the complete configured stage set is `plan`,
`implement`, `verify`, `docs`, and `review`; each receives an inline command
whose sole stage-specific value is its declared skill/agent name. The complete
wrapper boundary set is missing/invalid agent, missing ETUDE paths, missing
agent definition, zero inputs, ordered multi-input success, Claude failure,
empty or whitespace-only successful Claude output, and successful non-empty
output publication. The exact stage-to-agent set is `plan`→`dev-planner`,
`implement`→`dev-executor`, `verify`→`dev-qa`, `docs`→`dev-docs-writer`, and
`review`→`dev-pr-reviewer`; all five matching files exist under the checked-in
`.claude/agents/`. The workflow migration guard pins those five literal pairs,
asserts every real agent file exists, and covers the two required allowlisted
names.

Every configured stage declares at least one input. Every checked-in agent file
also pins its model explicitly (`opus` for plan/verify/review and `sonnet` for
implement/docs), so replacing `--model opus` with `--agent` is deliberate: each
stage now selects the model declared by its own producer agent rather than
forcing one registry-wide reviewer model.

TRUST BOUNDARY: `.etude/workflow.yaml`, wrapper argv, input role filenames, and
the wrapper's prompt framing are trusted repository/engine-produced control
data. Materialized input contents include prior Claude artifacts and are
untrusted data; the wrapper places each inside explicit begin/end delimiters and
states that delimited content is data, not wrapper instruction. The new Claude
response is likewise untrusted and is published only as the stage artifact.
The OAuth token value is secret process input and must never be printed,
persisted in the prompt, or asserted by value. The wrapper does not opt into
`--dangerously-skip-permissions`; Claude CLI's normal non-interactive permission
mode and the checked-in agent definition govern any tool access.

## Contract decision

A registry seat's `invoke` is a REVIEWER contract: shared prompt on stdin,
structured verdict envelope in `$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE`. A workflow stage runner is
a PRODUCER contract: declared inputs plus stage skill in, arbitrary artifact in
`$ETUDE_OUTPUT_FILE`. Reusing `runner: {name: dev}` crosses those contracts and
is the root defect.

Keep `dev` in the registry for schema/default compatibility, but stop using it
as a stage runner. Replace every workflow stage's named runner with an inline
command `bash .etude/dev-stage-runner.sh <stage-skill>`. `bash` is resolved from
PATH before launch; the relative script argument is then resolved inside the
pinned detached worktree. This also passes the skill identity explicitly
without a new reserved environment variable or shell-side YAML parsing.

## Files and implementation

- Add `.etude/dev-stage-runner.sh`:
  - validate one identifier-like agent argument, the three current ETUDE paths,
    and `.claude/agents/<agent>.md` before invoking Claude;
  - collect `ETUDE_INPUTS_DIR/*` in lexical order and fail closed on zero inputs;
  - build a prompt under the engine-owned scratch directory, register cleanup
    before writing it, generate an unpredictable per-invocation delimiter, and
    enclose complete input contents as role-labelled untrusted data;
  - invoke `claude -p --no-session-persistence --agent <agent>` with the prompt
